export function coupledGroundHTML(data){const g=data.coupledGround;return `<section class="qe-ground" aria-labelledby="qe-ground-title"><div class="qe-tag">All native sectors checked · explicit source design</div><h4 id="qe-ground-title">Which state really has the lowest energy?</h4><p>Checking only the particle code gives the wrong answer. In the uncompleted field extension, a neutral native excitation outside that code costs less than every interacting code state. The full-sector audit detects this.</p><div class="qe-ground-choices"><div><span>Uncompleted Hamiltonian</span><b>96 lowest active states outside the code</b><p>One native triplet can sit in any of 32 constituents. The code’s internal gap does not make its vacuum the ground of the whole source.</p></div><div><span>Chosen positive macrocell completion</span><b>One interacting code ground</b><p>Add m c(g)(I − Pₓ) at each macrocell. Its coefficient comes from source-only vacuum contractions. The code energies stay unchanged; non-code sectors cost more.</p></div></div><p class="qe-equation">H<sub>completed</sub> = H<sub>original</sub> + m c(g) ∑ₓ(I − Pₓ)</p><p>This is an explicit architecture choice, not a theorem that primitive repair forces that choice. It makes no fitted mass or hidden energy subtraction. Every original count history that preserves the local code patterns has exactly the same probabilities. Energies and phases between those patterns do change.</p><div class="qe-stats"><span><b>4 trials · 3 filters</b>every partner and failure is retained</span><span><b>≥ ${lowerPercent(g.preparationSuccessLower.display[0])}% success</b>for the complete preparation tree</span><span><b>&lt; 1.418 × 10<sup>−7</sup> state error</b>relative to the actual interacting ground</span></div><div class="qe-ground-record"><b>A ground can contain virtual dressing</b><p>The complete detector records a charge, its conjugate and one field quantum with probability at least <strong>${(Math.floor(g.preparedStationaryCountLower.display[0]*1e12)/1e6).toFixed(6)} per million</strong> complete preparation-and-readout attempts. These counts use the chosen free-particle basis. They do <strong>not</strong> describe radiation escaping a stationary ground.</p></div><details><summary>Energy, capacity and the remaining spatial requirement</summary><p>The full physical gap exceeds 2.52078 × 10<sup>−10</sup> source-energy units. It is different from the much larger gap within the code, and neither number is an identified electron mass. The retained preparation registers require fewer than 42,006.105 nats, plus the final count pointer; control-synthesis workspace is separate. The finite filters use the same raw Hamiltonian without an unknown exact ground projector or ground-energy phase.</p><p>The added penalty is local to each declared macrocell. The earlier kinetic/current construction still has a global code guard. A full-source spatial propagation theorem and a particle mass readout require additional analysis.</p></details><a href="${data.evidence.native_wilson_maxwell_ground.proof}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Complete ground audit, positive completion and prepared records ↗</a></section>`;}
